{"agent":"j1-kilo","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"Liveness is no longer read from the leases clock this function already used. Given a caller or test that freezes `_leases.time.time` (the injection the deleted call site used) at T while `CLOCK.wall()` is T+grace+1, `lease.live` is false and the function returns None for a lease the rest of the lease path still treats as live; the opposite freeze keeps a wall-expired lease. Queue/scratch/inbox now share the adapter clock, so this call is the one that disagrees.","digest":"e7e8f4c2c3a03246","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/leases/lifecycle.py:394","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/leases/lifecycle.py:394"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"`clock: Callable[[], float] = CLOCK.monotonic` evaluates `CLOCK.monotonic` once, at import, and stores that bound method as the default. A later swap of the adapter (the reason this change exists) does not affect callers that omit `clock`. Given `remote.CLOCK` replaced by a fake whose `monotonic` returns `0.0`, then `sample = timed(run, probe=probe)` with no `clock=` argument, the default still invokes the real monotonic captured at import, so the sample's timestamps are wall monotonic time, not `0.0`. `store.py` looks up `CLOCK.wall` when the store is built; this default does not. Patching `CLOCK.monotonic` on the same object also fails: the default already holds the old bound method.","digest":"7d8c05cfea027772","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/perf/remote.py:90","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/perf/remote.py:90"}],"chunks_total":3,"combined_gates":["rubber_duck","critic"],"coverage":"3/3 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":21511,"diff_sha":"fda726ae9de80af986cc4bac77a93312769118de441f9f4bbb608187bb5c8415","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/fix-Bc23ce3dd38","diff